WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.440
The first film showed how many-celled organisms share life's jobs.

2
00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:11.480
How can their cells become different enough to do those jobs?

3
00:00:12.583 --> 00:00:16.523
A specialized cell has a structure suited to a particular job.

4
00:00:18.003 --> 00:00:19.203
Form is not decoration.

5
00:00:20.463 --> 00:00:23.243
It changes what the cell can reach, carry, or move.

6
00:00:24.267 --> 00:00:26.727
A neuron grows a long, thin axon.

7
00:00:27.827 --> 00:00:30.227
It can carry a signal from the spine to the foot.

8
00:00:31.547 --> 00:00:33.427
Its reach fits long-distance signalling.

9
00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:39.900
A skeletal muscle cell is a long cylinder packed with fibres that shorten together.

10
00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:46.060
A root hair cell grows a thin tube into soil, adding contact where water enters.

11
00:00:47.183 --> 00:00:49.003
Structure can affect survival.

12
00:00:50.323 --> 00:00:55.763
In Arabidopsis seedlings, lines with longer root hairs stayed anchored better after

13
00:00:55.763 --> 00:01:00.103
simulated rain. Their main roots were about the same size.

14
00:01:01.323 --> 00:01:03.523
So root hair length is the likely reason.

15
00:01:04.567 --> 00:01:07.067
Now compare two red blood cell cartoons.

16
00:01:08.447 --> 00:01:09.547
One is a stiff sphere.

17
00:01:10.787 --> 00:01:13.647
The other is a flexible disc dented in the middle.

18
00:01:14.887 --> 00:01:16.267
Which passes a narrow vessel?

19
00:01:22.783 --> 00:01:25.723
The flexible dented disc bends and squeezes through.

20
00:01:26.923 --> 00:01:29.783
A mature red blood cell also has no nucleus.

21
00:01:31.163 --> 00:01:34.583
Its small flexible form suits transport through narrow vessels.

22
00:01:35.717 --> 00:01:37.997
Different shapes raise a deeper question.

23
00:01:39.337 --> 00:01:42.117
Do nerve and muscle cells carry different instructions?

24
00:01:43.657 --> 00:01:50.257
Mostly no. Nearly every body cell with a nucleus carries essentially the same DNA.

25
00:01:51.567 --> 00:01:56.787
What differs is gene expression, the use of a gene's instructions to make a product.

26
00:01:58.127 --> 00:02:00.667
Each kind of cell uses a different set of genes.

27
00:02:02.287 --> 00:02:06.147
Cells fit life's jobs through structure and gene use, alone or together.

28
00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:11.500
A muscle cell uses genes for proteins that slide and shorten it.

29
00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:13.880
Other genes stay unused.

30
00:02:14.933 --> 00:02:20.173
A developing red blood cell uses genes for hemoglobin, the protein that carries

31
00:02:20.173 --> 00:02:23.733
oxygen. Later it pushes out its nucleus.

32
00:02:25.173 --> 00:02:28.533
Its earlier gene use helped build its specialized contents.

33
00:02:29.583 --> 00:02:32.783
This is a main model, not a claim without exceptions.

34
00:02:34.203 --> 00:02:35.983
Some immune cells rearrange DNA.

35
00:02:37.683 --> 00:02:42.243
Egg and sperm cells carry one chromosome set, while most body cells carry two.

36
00:02:43.283 --> 00:02:45.083
Experiments support the model.

37
00:02:46.363 --> 00:02:51.403
A few frog gut-cell nuclei, moved into eggs, still built tadpoles with nerve,

38
00:02:51.823 --> 00:02:52.863
muscle, and blood cells.

39
00:02:54.263 --> 00:02:58.323
In those nuclei, specializing had not thrown the unused genes away.

40
00:02:59.417 --> 00:03:03.137
So cell differences need not mean different instruction books.

41
00:03:04.437 --> 00:03:08.777
Different cells use different parts of a shared book, building structures that suit

42
00:03:08.777 --> 00:03:09.297
their work.
